Comb Filter...\

\b0 This brings up a panel which lets you perform convolution over the sound.  If you don't know what this is, it won't help you much probably.  Even if you 
\i do
\i0  know, it won't help much as the panel provides no automatic convolution-kernel generation facilities, sorry.  This is primarily provided as an example and educational tool.  FIR filters (the common types) use the 
\b Non-Recursive
\b0  setting.  IIR (recursive) filters use the 
\b Recursive
\b0  setting.  Press 
\b Apply
\b0  to begin the convolution process over the 
\i entire
\i0  sound, not the sound selection.  The filter automatically normalizes the results.\
\

\b Echo Filter...\

\b0 This brings up a panel which lets you add echos into your sound.  To add an echo 
\i n
\i0  samples in the future (where one second is 44100 samples), add a row with 
\i n
\i0  in the position column, and set the amplitude column to how loud the echo should be (expressed as a fraction, between 0 and 1, of the current sound amplitude).  To put the echo 
\i n
\i0  samples in the past (that is, prior to the sound it's echoing), make the position column equal to negative 
\i n.  
\i0 \
\
Unless for some reason you want 
\i only
\i0  echos, you'll probably want the original sound mixed in with your echos.  To do this, leave the first row (position 0, amplitude 1) untouched–it will represent the original sound.  Note: You'll probably not want your echos (and original sound) amplitude fractions summing to more than 1.
